  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.” The two replies are as follows. Reply A is the text: ```xml\n{\"alpha\": 1}\n```\n{\"beta\": 2}. Reply B is the text: {\"beta\": 2}. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"alpha\": 1}, because the ```xml fence holds an object and it is the last fenced object. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {\"beta\": 2}, because no fence holds an object, so the last top-level object is scored. The check, however, returns {\"beta\": 2} for both replies: its _FENCE regex only recognizes ```json, ```JSON, or an unlabeled fence, so it ignores the ```xml fence in Reply A and takes the last top-level object, which is {\"beta\": 2}. This makes the check give the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check actually returns {\"alpha\": 1} for Reply A, or if the rule is read as applying only to fences labeled json or unlabeled, in which case the ```xml fence would not count and the rule would also require {\"beta\": 2} for Reply A."
